UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 24, Lesson 1: Theme and the author''s message', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Theme is a bigger idea a text suggests (not just the topic); readers infer it from patterns of events and support it with evidence.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhich is closer to theme than topic: (a) ferries (b) honesty rebuilds trust after a mistake?\n\nFEEDBACK\n(b) is a bigger idea; (a) is a topic label.\n\nCHECK\nA theme stated with no link to events is a guess, not an inference.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPropose two possible themes for a short story. Support each with two quoted events and choose the stronger fit.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Theme is a bigger idea a text suggests (not just the topic); readers infer it from patterns of events and support it with evidence.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01","EN2-UARL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Propose two possible themes for a short story. Support each with two quoted events and choose the stronger fit. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using theme on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: A theme stated with no link to events is a guess, not an inference.. Correction: (b) is a bigger idea; (a) is a topic label.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A theme stated with no link to events is a guess, not an inference. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating theme on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with theme, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about theme with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for theme. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for theme. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["(b) is a bigger idea; (a) is a topic label","A theme stated with no link to events is a guess, not an inference","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Which is closer to theme than topic: (a) ferries (b) honesty rebuilds trust after a mistake?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for theme; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Theme is a bigger idea a text suggests (not just the topic)","(b) is a bigger idea; (a) is a topic label","Careful use of message and explain why it matters with clear evidence","A theme stated with no link to events is a guess, not an inference"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named message and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A theme stated with no link to events is a guess, not an inference","Name message and explain why it matters only, without linking it to theme","Apply theme to new material and name the message and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies theme with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries theme. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A theme stated with no link to events is a guess, not an inference","Point to exact words in the sentence that show theme and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words"],"question":"Read: \"After the blackout, Tess lit a candle and read the crumpled note.\" Which choice best uses theme from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name message and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","(b) is a bigger idea; (a) is a topic label","A theme stated with no link to events is a guess, not an inference","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse message and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new","A theme stated with no link to events is a guess, not an inference","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about theme","Retype the worked model as the final answer"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of theme on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about theme with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating theme on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using theme on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for theme. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with theme, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: A theme stated with no link to events is a guess, not an inference.. Correction: (b) is a bigger idea; (a) is a topic label.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w24-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise theme.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail h